WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:08.160 --> 00:00:11.170
- Welcome to the Yale Council
on Southeast Asian Studies,

00:00:11.170 --> 00:00:13.190
virtual Brown Bag Series.

00:00:13.190 --> 00:00:14.920
My name is Erik Harms

00:00:14.920 --> 00:00:17.170
and I'm the chair of the council.

00:00:17.170 --> 00:00:20.010
And it's a pleasure to be
moderating today's discussion

00:00:20.010 --> 00:00:23.040
with Nicole CuUnjieng Aboitiz

00:00:23.040 --> 00:00:26.200
one of our own recent PhD graduates.

00:00:26.200 --> 00:00:27.520
Before we begin,

00:00:27.520 --> 00:00:28.410
I need to just note

00:00:28.410 --> 00:00:31.140
a few minor bureaucratic
and technical details.

00:00:31.140 --> 00:00:34.200
First, I just clicked the record button.

00:00:34.200 --> 00:00:35.780
So you should all know that this session

00:00:35.780 --> 00:00:38.920
is being recorded for archival purposes.

00:00:38.920 --> 00:00:41.240
If you do not wish to be recorded,

00:00:41.240 --> 00:00:43.540
please turn off your video camera.

00:00:43.540 --> 00:00:45.730
And if you truly do not
wish to be identified

00:00:45.730 --> 00:00:47.750
as being a part of this session,

00:00:47.750 --> 00:00:50.240
you might also consider
changing your screen name

00:00:50.240 --> 00:00:51.733
in the Zoom interface.

00:00:53.210 --> 00:00:57.620
Second, we ask that all
participants keep their audio muted,

00:00:57.620 --> 00:01:00.130
unless you are a
designated as the speaker,

00:01:00.130 --> 00:01:02.860
or if during the question
and answer period,

00:01:02.860 --> 00:01:04.050
you have some questions

00:01:04.050 --> 00:01:06.003
at the end of the panel presentation.

00:01:07.270 --> 00:01:09.540
So just to note,

00:01:09.540 --> 00:01:11.080
this is being recorded.

00:01:11.080 --> 00:01:12.720
And if you're not interested

00:01:12.720 --> 00:01:15.130
in having your image on that thing up,

00:01:15.130 --> 00:01:16.363
turn off your camera.

00:01:18.360 --> 00:01:19.270
So I'm really excited

00:01:19.270 --> 00:01:23.170
to welcome Nicole CuUnjieng Aboitiz today

00:01:23.170 --> 00:01:24.680
because it brings back memories

00:01:24.680 --> 00:01:26.900
of an intensive Southeast Asia seminar

00:01:26.900 --> 00:01:30.710
I taught in the fall of 2013 here at Yale.

00:01:30.710 --> 00:01:32.430
Nicole was in that course,

00:01:32.430 --> 00:01:35.160
and at the time she was then experimenting

00:01:35.160 --> 00:01:36.930
with some of the ideas that would go on

00:01:36.930 --> 00:01:39.240
to inform her dissertation research.

00:01:39.240 --> 00:01:41.297
And now this book,

00:01:41.297 --> 00:01:44.010
"Asian Place Filipino Nation",

00:01:44.010 --> 00:01:46.840
which I recommend you all
run out and buy right away.

00:01:46.840 --> 00:01:48.423
It's a really fantastic book.

00:01:49.870 --> 00:01:51.440
But it's really exciting reading the book

00:01:51.440 --> 00:01:54.350
because it brings back
memories of that class.

00:01:54.350 --> 00:01:57.300
I distinctly remember learning
so much from her at that time

00:01:57.300 --> 00:02:00.530
about our interest in Jose P Laurel,

00:02:00.530 --> 00:02:02.780
a graduate of the Yale Law School

00:02:02.780 --> 00:02:04.110
who went on to become the president

00:02:04.110 --> 00:02:06.340
of the second Republic of the Philippines

00:02:06.340 --> 00:02:08.210
right after the Japanese

00:02:08.210 --> 00:02:10.490
and Laurel learned a lot from Yale.

00:02:10.490 --> 00:02:11.940
But what was so interesting

00:02:11.940 --> 00:02:13.850
in what Nicole taught me so much about

00:02:13.850 --> 00:02:16.270
was the way in which he
borrowed from the West,

00:02:16.270 --> 00:02:18.410
but he never fetishized the West.

00:02:18.410 --> 00:02:20.140
And he was much more interested

00:02:20.140 --> 00:02:22.290
in what he called mother Asia,

00:02:22.290 --> 00:02:23.500
which was cultivated through

00:02:23.500 --> 00:02:26.480
his long-term engagement with Japan.

00:02:26.480 --> 00:02:27.640
And ever since then,

00:02:27.640 --> 00:02:30.660
I've been really impressed
by the breadth of knowledge

00:02:30.660 --> 00:02:32.380
that Nicole brings to the table,

00:02:32.380 --> 00:02:34.260
not only about the Philippines,

00:02:34.260 --> 00:02:36.480
but about all of Southeast Asia.

00:02:36.480 --> 00:02:38.950
And it's a kind of breath that recalls

00:02:38.950 --> 00:02:41.850
the capacious and comparative
thinking of my colleague,

00:02:41.850 --> 00:02:42.850
Ben Kiernan,

00:02:42.850 --> 00:02:44.220
who was her advisor.

00:02:44.220 --> 00:02:45.700
It's really fascinating .

00:02:45.700 --> 00:02:47.540
The first time I ever met Ben

00:02:47.540 --> 00:02:50.700
is just having conversations about history

00:02:50.700 --> 00:02:52.700
and the way he would
draw in so many threads

00:02:52.700 --> 00:02:54.630
from all across the world,

00:02:54.630 --> 00:02:56.600
into these weaving these tails,

00:02:56.600 --> 00:02:59.680
sometimes just walking
through the new Haven green,

00:02:59.680 --> 00:03:01.570
all of these stories would come together

00:03:01.570 --> 00:03:04.240
and it's that same kind
of passion for history

00:03:04.240 --> 00:03:06.060
and bringing together all these threads

00:03:06.060 --> 00:03:08.620
that I see in Nicole's work as well.

00:03:08.620 --> 00:03:11.370
And it's that breadth of
thinking that has allowed her

00:03:11.370 --> 00:03:13.560
to rethink the intellectual engagements

00:03:13.560 --> 00:03:15.420
of the Philippine revolution,

00:03:15.420 --> 00:03:18.430
in ways that look to enter Asian exchanges

00:03:18.430 --> 00:03:22.420
in a sort of Pan-Asianism
that dissenters the West.

00:03:22.420 --> 00:03:25.280
I'm proud to say that
Nicole CuUnjieng Aboitiz

00:03:25.280 --> 00:03:26.960
is currently a Research Fellow

00:03:26.960 --> 00:03:29.480
at Clare Hall, University of Cambridge,

00:03:29.480 --> 00:03:33.450
and is Executive Director of
the Toynbee Prize Foundation.

00:03:33.450 --> 00:03:36.010
She's truly one of our most accomplished

00:03:36.010 --> 00:03:37.713
graduates of recent years.

00:03:38.570 --> 00:03:41.200
So thank you for coming
back to Yale, Nicole,

00:03:41.200 --> 00:03:44.495
to our Zoom edition of
the Brown Bag Series.

00:03:44.495 --> 00:03:47.040
Everyone, please join me in welcoming her.

00:03:47.040 --> 00:03:48.970
And then I'll say a word or two about

00:03:49.949 --> 00:03:52.570
the organization of today's discussion.

00:03:52.570 --> 00:03:55.040
So just take a moment to welcome Nicole.

00:03:55.040 --> 00:03:56.763
Thank you so much for joining us.

00:03:58.240 --> 00:04:01.220
Now, the way Nicole and I decided

00:04:01.220 --> 00:04:04.050
to run this particular session,

00:04:04.050 --> 00:04:05.853
given the Zoom format,

00:04:06.800 --> 00:04:08.000
we thought that rather than

00:04:08.000 --> 00:04:10.160
a kind of long extended monologue

00:04:10.160 --> 00:04:13.340
of the classical book talk style,

00:04:13.340 --> 00:04:16.550
that we would engage in
a kind of conversation,

00:04:16.550 --> 00:04:19.020
a moderated discussion,

00:04:19.020 --> 00:04:21.020
a kind of question and answer

00:04:21.020 --> 00:04:24.640
maybe a Southeast Asian studies
version of the daily show

00:04:24.640 --> 00:04:29.640
when we have a guest who's
just written a fantastic book.

00:04:29.700 --> 00:04:32.050
So I'll start that off
with the first question.

00:04:32.050 --> 00:04:33.720
After I've asked a few questions,

00:04:33.720 --> 00:04:36.860
I'm sure many of you will
have your own questions.

00:04:36.860 --> 00:04:38.380
And so if you start having questions,

00:04:38.380 --> 00:04:41.640
just please use the raise
hand function on Zoom,

00:04:41.640 --> 00:04:43.210
or just start each shouting

00:04:43.210 --> 00:04:45.470
if you can't get my attention

00:04:45.470 --> 00:04:46.810
and feel free to interrupt,

00:04:46.810 --> 00:04:49.410
because we want this to
be a dynamic conversation

00:04:49.410 --> 00:04:52.020
about what's truly a fascinating book.

00:04:52.020 --> 00:04:55.740
So, let me start with the
first question for you, Nicole.

00:04:55.740 --> 00:04:59.870
You talk in the book about
de-centering the West

00:04:59.870 --> 00:05:01.700
and refocusing attention

00:05:01.700 --> 00:05:04.380
on what you've called East East relations.

00:05:04.380 --> 00:05:07.250
What you also call Pan-Asianism.

00:05:07.250 --> 00:05:09.690
Can you explain what
led you to this approach

00:05:09.690 --> 00:05:11.760
and why you think it's important

00:05:11.760 --> 00:05:13.240
and how you think it contributes

00:05:13.240 --> 00:05:15.480
to the field of Asian studies,

00:05:15.480 --> 00:05:17.623
Southeast Asian studies and so on?

00:05:19.420 --> 00:05:20.253
- Yes, of course.

00:05:20.253 --> 00:05:24.560
So, the late 19th and early 20th century,

00:05:24.560 --> 00:05:28.040
is such a dynamic and important

00:05:28.040 --> 00:05:30.010
crucial turning point

00:05:30.010 --> 00:05:32.420
of knowing global history
and Imperial history,

00:05:32.420 --> 00:05:34.610
especially in Southeast Asia history.

00:05:34.610 --> 00:05:37.300
And you see throughout this period,

00:05:37.300 --> 00:05:39.800
the entire map of Southeast Asia

00:05:39.800 --> 00:05:44.240
undergoing quite strong
Imperial consolidation

00:05:44.240 --> 00:05:48.920
and experiencing local
resistance at the same time.

00:05:48.920 --> 00:05:52.540
And this is true across the entire map.

00:05:52.540 --> 00:05:56.810
And it also happens to be
the foundational moment

00:05:56.810 --> 00:05:58.890
for Philippine history

00:05:58.890 --> 00:06:00.930
because it's the founding
of the Philippine nation

00:06:00.930 --> 00:06:02.790
and the eventual nation state.

00:06:02.790 --> 00:06:07.610
And you have the first stories
of anti-colonial nationalism

00:06:07.610 --> 00:06:09.620
in the late 19th century,

00:06:09.620 --> 00:06:12.180
as well as the creation of the first

00:06:12.180 --> 00:06:14.950
modern secular Republic in all of Asia

00:06:14.950 --> 00:06:16.950
in the form of the first
Philippine Republic

00:06:16.950 --> 00:06:18.950
though it's short lived.

00:06:18.950 --> 00:06:23.170
And it's also the transition
between the old world,

00:06:23.170 --> 00:06:28.050
European Spanish Imperial
power to the new world,

00:06:28.050 --> 00:06:31.220
American colonial period,

00:06:31.220 --> 00:06:34.770
as well as the emergence of Japan in Asia.

00:06:34.770 --> 00:06:39.657
And yet the discussion of
this foundational moment

00:06:39.657 --> 00:06:41.940
and Philippine history

00:06:41.940 --> 00:06:43.740
treats it as if it took place

00:06:43.740 --> 00:06:46.370
in a completely different
part of the world

00:06:46.370 --> 00:06:49.640
entirely without mention of Asia at all.

00:06:49.640 --> 00:06:53.430
And it sort of underscores what
is a longstanding assumption

00:06:53.430 --> 00:06:56.800
within Philippine history
that the Filipino self-image

00:06:56.800 --> 00:06:59.640
is historically non-Asians seeing itself

00:06:59.640 --> 00:07:01.590
as belonging to the Western hemisphere.

00:07:02.790 --> 00:07:06.040
And it seemed to me, frankly,

00:07:06.040 --> 00:07:08.620
impossible that the Philippines
would have been divorced

00:07:08.620 --> 00:07:10.800
from all of this tumult in Asia,

00:07:10.800 --> 00:07:13.500
both Southeast Asia and
East Asia at the time.

00:07:13.500 --> 00:07:15.650
And it seemed to me, frankly,

00:07:15.650 --> 00:07:18.600
Eurocentric or at best
Imperial to believe that

00:07:18.600 --> 00:07:20.030
this would have been the case

00:07:20.030 --> 00:07:23.120
that we've been so perfectly
isolated from the rest.

00:07:23.120 --> 00:07:26.740
And this is an assumption
that I saw reproduced

00:07:26.740 --> 00:07:30.010
outside the specifically
Philippine literature

00:07:30.010 --> 00:07:33.580
and also within the larger
Southeast Asian literature.

00:07:33.580 --> 00:07:35.300
So I wanted to interrogate this

00:07:36.860 --> 00:07:41.200
and I had when I just started at Yale

00:07:41.200 --> 00:07:43.573
recently read Cemil Aydin's book on

00:07:43.573 --> 00:07:46.700
the Politics of anti-Westernism in Asia.

00:07:46.700 --> 00:07:50.230
And this idea about the contestation

00:07:50.230 --> 00:07:52.510
of a single Eurocentric world order

00:07:53.790 --> 00:07:56.530
was really enlightening to me.

00:07:56.530 --> 00:08:00.150
And the idea that Asia was the framework

00:08:00.150 --> 00:08:04.990
in which a lot of Asian nationalisms

00:08:04.990 --> 00:08:07.750
were arraying themselves

00:08:07.750 --> 00:08:10.210
against or would be

00:08:10.210 --> 00:08:13.270
Western had Germany

00:08:13.270 --> 00:08:16.200
or European hierarchy of races

00:08:16.200 --> 00:08:18.630
was a really interesting
thread to me to see

00:08:19.570 --> 00:08:22.130
and test whether that was true

00:08:22.130 --> 00:08:25.230
in the Philippines too
perhaps in the beginning,

00:08:25.230 --> 00:08:26.620
if not so much at the end.

00:08:26.620 --> 00:08:28.690
And what I found instead was that

00:08:28.690 --> 00:08:31.650
Asia becomes this sort of blank canvas

00:08:31.650 --> 00:08:34.380
upon which you can project
your fantasies and fears,

00:08:34.380 --> 00:08:36.780
and it can track and index

00:08:36.780 --> 00:08:40.520
sort of prevailing paradigm shifts

00:08:41.560 --> 00:08:45.490
and was an interesting way to look at

00:08:45.490 --> 00:08:47.040
both international vectors

00:08:47.040 --> 00:08:50.770
as well as sort of vernacular
vocabularies of power

00:08:50.770 --> 00:08:52.077
that co-constituted

00:08:53.120 --> 00:08:57.460
ideas of Asia across
the sort of late 19th,

00:08:57.460 --> 00:08:58.583
early 20th century.

00:09:04.805 --> 00:09:05.638
- Thanks, Nicole.

00:09:05.638 --> 00:09:07.010
That's fascinating.

00:09:07.010 --> 00:09:08.350
You're a historian

00:09:08.350 --> 00:09:11.480
and I know through my
conversations with historians,

00:09:11.480 --> 00:09:13.470
that one thing that historians

00:09:13.470 --> 00:09:15.580
love to talk about is the archives.

00:09:15.580 --> 00:09:20.130
And often these aha moments

00:09:20.130 --> 00:09:22.170
kind of emerge in the archive.

00:09:22.170 --> 00:09:23.930
And I wondered if you could share with us

00:09:23.930 --> 00:09:27.100
a moment where you sort of you decided,

00:09:27.100 --> 00:09:29.080
or you were combing through the archive

00:09:29.080 --> 00:09:31.120
or something that jumped
out and spoke to you

00:09:31.120 --> 00:09:34.750
from the archive that
almost set you on this path

00:09:35.750 --> 00:09:38.880
and towards this larger
argument that you're making,

00:09:38.880 --> 00:09:42.330
and maybe describe a
figure or a piece of paper

00:09:42.330 --> 00:09:44.280
that really just brought it out to you.

00:09:46.470 --> 00:09:48.590
- Well, a lot of my
dissertation advisors are here,

00:09:48.590 --> 00:09:49.590
so they'll enjoy this.

00:09:49.590 --> 00:09:54.460
But I was told when I was first giving

00:09:54.460 --> 00:09:56.590
my dissertation perspectives that

00:09:56.590 --> 00:09:58.960
the connection between the Philippines

00:09:58.960 --> 00:10:01.790
and Pan-Asianism could
be at best a chapter,

00:10:01.790 --> 00:10:03.650
but not an entire book.

00:10:03.650 --> 00:10:06.600
And so I went off to the archives

00:10:06.600 --> 00:10:08.200
having given everyone this

00:10:08.200 --> 00:10:10.010
big dissertation perspective about

00:10:10.010 --> 00:10:12.210
the intellectual history of
the Philippine revolution,

00:10:12.210 --> 00:10:16.000
in which Pan-Asianism was
just one chapter or Asianism,

00:10:16.000 --> 00:10:18.810
Asianism Paniism we're one chapter.

00:10:18.810 --> 00:10:20.520
And I didn't tell them,

00:10:20.520 --> 00:10:24.910
but I went out to set to
prove them wrong, essentially.

00:10:24.910 --> 00:10:26.120
And when I was in the archives,

00:10:26.120 --> 00:10:29.030
I just found so much that

00:10:30.090 --> 00:10:33.240
of the sort of traditional sources

00:10:34.090 --> 00:10:36.600
in which all of the references to Asia

00:10:36.600 --> 00:10:39.970
and all of the more specific
particular lies, understanding.

00:10:39.970 --> 00:10:42.940
So race as a rising
from climate and place,

00:10:42.940 --> 00:10:47.460
all these very specific
things had been left aside.

00:10:47.460 --> 00:10:49.900
And that was not what
was discussed in all of

00:10:51.197 --> 00:10:53.186
the sort of the traditional
works on for Series-A

00:10:53.186 --> 00:10:54.380
or beanie

00:10:54.380 --> 00:10:56.280
all the big figures
that Philippine history.

00:10:56.280 --> 00:10:58.060
So I didn't even have to go that far

00:10:58.060 --> 00:11:00.410
within the archives to make my point.

00:11:00.410 --> 00:11:03.491
And I basically didn't tell
my advisors what I was doing

00:11:03.491 --> 00:11:06.920
until I had written the whole
practically the whole thing.

00:11:06.920 --> 00:11:09.680
And I showed them, see, there
is enough material actually,

00:11:09.680 --> 00:11:12.550
to do a whole dissertation.

00:11:12.550 --> 00:11:14.180
And by then they couldn't argue with me.

00:11:14.180 --> 00:11:18.303
So what I presented as a
chapter became the whole work.

00:11:23.644 --> 00:11:26.644
(sound fading away)

00:11:28.450 --> 00:11:29.870
- No, please go ahead.

00:11:29.870 --> 00:11:32.270
- I'm just saying you wanted
something a bit more specific.

00:11:32.270 --> 00:11:33.770
So I can talk about one thing,

00:11:33.770 --> 00:11:37.400
which was a series of talks about how

00:11:38.240 --> 00:11:41.220
Filipinos had forgotten their own songs,

00:11:41.220 --> 00:11:42.240
their own poetry,

00:11:42.240 --> 00:11:43.530
their own place

00:11:43.530 --> 00:11:47.230
to learn the songs and poetry of others

00:11:47.230 --> 00:11:51.640
that were not consonant with
their own ways of thinking

00:11:51.640 --> 00:11:55.213
and brought about by their climate.

00:11:56.120 --> 00:11:58.040
And that was one moment where I saw

00:11:58.040 --> 00:12:01.250
a way in which they were the Hills Placedo

00:12:01.250 --> 00:12:02.590
and his contemporaries,

00:12:02.590 --> 00:12:06.800
were theorizing race as tied to place

00:12:06.800 --> 00:12:09.340
and it naturalized base.

00:12:09.340 --> 00:12:11.884
And then also made it inalienable

00:12:11.884 --> 00:12:14.220
from the actual archipelago,

00:12:14.220 --> 00:12:16.800
from the particular
evolutionary thread there,

00:12:16.800 --> 00:12:18.760
the climate there.

00:12:18.760 --> 00:12:21.270
And I thought that
showed me a way in which

00:12:21.270 --> 00:12:23.860
I could start to look at
a more multi-dimensional

00:12:23.860 --> 00:12:26.142
understanding of what Asia meant

00:12:26.142 --> 00:12:28.530
in Philippine intellectual history

00:12:28.530 --> 00:12:30.650
beyond merely a sort of political

00:12:30.650 --> 00:12:32.573
social Darwinist, real politic,

00:12:33.490 --> 00:12:35.680
transnational solidarity.

00:12:35.680 --> 00:12:37.920
I thought that there was a larger,

00:12:37.920 --> 00:12:40.710
more sort of complex grounding to Asianism

00:12:40.710 --> 00:12:44.040
than just a more narrow Pan-Asianism.

00:12:44.040 --> 00:12:47.090
Though that Pan-Asianism
was also a revision

00:12:47.090 --> 00:12:49.527
to our understanding of
the Philippine revolution

00:12:49.527 --> 00:12:50.773
and Philippine history.

00:12:53.470 --> 00:12:54.744
- Thanks, Nicole.

00:12:54.744 --> 00:12:57.758
I wanna continue in this
thread a little bit.

00:12:57.758 --> 00:13:01.040
There's a theme about the
idea of Southeast Asia

00:13:01.040 --> 00:13:03.970
in the introduction to your book,

00:13:03.970 --> 00:13:05.880
which I found really fascinating.

00:13:05.880 --> 00:13:08.620
And since this is a Brown bag
on Southeast Asian studies,

00:13:08.620 --> 00:13:11.650
I thought I'd ask you to
pursue that a little bit

00:13:11.650 --> 00:13:13.887
and explain it to the audience.

00:13:13.887 --> 00:13:14.893
So one of the funny things

00:13:14.893 --> 00:13:17.150
that we as Southeast Asianists do

00:13:17.150 --> 00:13:20.430
is we fight for the field
of Southeast Asian studies,

00:13:20.430 --> 00:13:21.660
but at the same time,

00:13:21.660 --> 00:13:24.010
we constantly tell the
world that Southeast Asia

00:13:24.010 --> 00:13:27.560
is an imaginary construction
of Western military

00:13:27.560 --> 00:13:29.880
and colonial imaginaries
and all of those things.

00:13:29.880 --> 00:13:32.290
And in the book you remind us of that,

00:13:32.290 --> 00:13:34.280
but then you also go on to say that

00:13:34.280 --> 00:13:36.773
there's something about
the Philippine revolution

00:13:36.773 --> 00:13:41.070
that reproduced and constructed
an idea of Southeast Asia

00:13:41.070 --> 00:13:44.090
and of Asian-ness inspired

00:13:44.090 --> 00:13:47.110
by this sense of shared Malayaness,

00:13:47.110 --> 00:13:49.300
which I found to be really fascinating.

00:13:49.300 --> 00:13:50.910
And I wondered if you could take some time

00:13:50.910 --> 00:13:53.160
to really explain that to the group,

00:13:53.160 --> 00:13:56.850
because I think it really
does some important work

00:13:56.850 --> 00:13:58.820
for the field of Southeast Asian studies

00:13:58.820 --> 00:14:01.110
and how we can imagine Southeast Asia,

00:14:01.110 --> 00:14:05.610
not only as a Relic of
Western power politics,

00:14:05.610 --> 00:14:08.333
but also from a different vantage point.

00:14:11.761 --> 00:14:14.680
- I worried a little bit in the beginning

00:14:14.680 --> 00:14:16.970
because I referred to Southeast Asia

00:14:16.970 --> 00:14:18.440
sort of anachronistically

00:14:18.440 --> 00:14:20.970
because of course the
concept didn't yet exist.

00:14:20.970 --> 00:14:24.520
And the historical subjects I look at,

00:14:24.520 --> 00:14:26.890
never refer to one another

00:14:26.890 --> 00:14:28.900
to their neighbors as Southeast Asians

00:14:28.900 --> 00:14:32.520
or fellow Malays or Asians,

00:14:32.520 --> 00:14:35.070
but more often they're
referred to by their

00:14:35.070 --> 00:14:36.270
sort of colonial overlord.

00:14:36.270 --> 00:14:40.160
So it's those under British colonialism,

00:14:40.160 --> 00:14:41.803
those under French colonialism.

00:14:43.030 --> 00:14:47.550
And I employ Southeast Asia as a heuristic

00:14:47.550 --> 00:14:49.870
and conceive of it as
a political construct.

00:14:49.870 --> 00:14:52.340
But I think that one of
the things we obtained

00:14:52.340 --> 00:14:56.290
by returning to sort of East
East history in this period,

00:14:56.290 --> 00:14:57.950
instead of the bilateral framework that

00:14:57.950 --> 00:15:00.070
we so often use for this period,

00:15:00.070 --> 00:15:02.370
is to see the creation of Southeast Asia

00:15:02.370 --> 00:15:05.850
as something other than merely
a by-product of the West.

00:15:05.850 --> 00:15:08.090
And you see the
anti-colonial consciousness

00:15:09.000 --> 00:15:12.060
and transnational pagination
thinking and engagement

00:15:12.060 --> 00:15:14.130
as a historical force that would go on

00:15:14.130 --> 00:15:18.873
to help scaffold the self-conscious

00:15:20.380 --> 00:15:22.290
region later on.

00:15:22.290 --> 00:15:23.520
So for that reason,

00:15:23.520 --> 00:15:26.663
I thought it made sense to
anachronistically referred to it.

00:15:29.260 --> 00:15:30.093
Yeah.

00:15:32.220 --> 00:15:34.280
- Great. Can you explain a little bit

00:15:34.280 --> 00:15:35.840
about the idea of Malayness

00:15:35.840 --> 00:15:39.440
and how this became so
central to the construction

00:15:39.440 --> 00:15:41.943
of new ideas of Philippine identity?

00:15:43.100 --> 00:15:45.260
- Yeah, it's a slippery one.

00:15:45.260 --> 00:15:47.230
I think even for them,

00:15:47.230 --> 00:15:49.910
for at least for the
instructors that I look at.

00:15:49.910 --> 00:15:50.743
But it was

00:15:53.690 --> 00:15:56.410
one thing that shows first how unstable

00:15:56.410 --> 00:16:00.630
the categories of race and
ethnicity were at the time

00:16:01.620 --> 00:16:06.620
and also how they could be
used to sort of buttress

00:16:09.250 --> 00:16:12.300
Asian nationalisms that were seemingly

00:16:12.300 --> 00:16:13.740
insufficient on their own.

00:16:13.740 --> 00:16:16.820
So, it becomes very
important to the Philippines

00:16:16.820 --> 00:16:18.670
to be counted as Malay

00:16:18.670 --> 00:16:20.660
so that they can counter the argument

00:16:20.660 --> 00:16:23.520
that they're just an
anarchy of tribes and races

00:16:23.520 --> 00:16:27.370
in an archipelago leftover and unmoored,

00:16:27.370 --> 00:16:31.080
not part of a larger sort of stage.

00:16:31.080 --> 00:16:34.380
And not only that,

00:16:34.380 --> 00:16:39.380
they then sort of explore
Japan's Malay origin

00:16:39.670 --> 00:16:42.480
and then being able to
claim Japan as Malay,

00:16:42.480 --> 00:16:46.930
they get to ride on the
coattails of Japanese power.

00:16:46.930 --> 00:16:49.440
Essentially they're much
more nonchalant toward

00:16:50.400 --> 00:16:53.920
Japanese power than say the
Chinese are at the time.

00:16:53.920 --> 00:16:56.010
And they use this,

00:16:56.010 --> 00:16:59.196
their construct of the Malay race

00:16:59.196 --> 00:17:02.874
as a way to separate
themselves from the Chinese

00:17:02.874 --> 00:17:05.123
and to claim the Japanese.

00:17:05.970 --> 00:17:07.620
So it's quite a malleable concept

00:17:12.142 --> 00:17:13.131
and they use a lot of the sort of

00:17:13.131 --> 00:17:16.940
European folklorist

00:17:16.940 --> 00:17:21.877
and anthropological or not
lot of the folklore studies

00:17:24.610 --> 00:17:29.460
to basically identify shared traits.

00:17:29.460 --> 00:17:31.599
And they look at the linguistic studies,

00:17:31.599 --> 00:17:33.910
Dutch linguistics studies,

00:17:33.910 --> 00:17:38.311
as well as German to basically
say back to the Europeans,

00:17:38.311 --> 00:17:43.311
this a fixed race that we are part of,

00:17:43.497 --> 00:17:45.010
and this is their entry point

00:17:45.010 --> 00:17:46.940
into a kind of universal

00:17:47.930 --> 00:17:50.040
history as they imagine it.

00:17:50.040 --> 00:17:52.890
So they can have a place within this

00:17:52.890 --> 00:17:55.010
sort of staged history.

00:17:55.010 --> 00:17:58.180
So, they take a sort of ranking of racists

00:17:58.180 --> 00:18:02.820
and their answer to a
racialized kind of denigration

00:18:02.820 --> 00:18:04.653
is through race itself.

00:18:07.688 --> 00:18:11.220
And in that way they use it
both positively and defensively.

00:18:11.220 --> 00:18:14.800
So, it becomes a defensive kind of

00:18:14.800 --> 00:18:17.210
social Darwinist strategy,

00:18:17.210 --> 00:18:19.250
a way to be part of something larger

00:18:20.210 --> 00:18:25.210
and to buttress your otherwise
localized nationalism

00:18:26.090 --> 00:18:28.530
in a larger transnational anti-colonial

00:18:28.530 --> 00:18:30.223
and racial solidarity.

00:18:31.360 --> 00:18:33.630
And it's also becomes a positive

00:18:33.630 --> 00:18:35.473
sort of act of world making.

00:18:36.410 --> 00:18:39.650
And they construct the Malay race

00:18:40.710 --> 00:18:44.160
to basically have a place
within an Asian landscape

00:18:44.160 --> 00:18:45.320
where they don't have

00:18:46.206 --> 00:18:48.860
sort of ancient ruins and other things

00:18:48.860 --> 00:18:50.730
to specify their nationalism.

00:18:50.730 --> 00:18:52.373
So they look outwards.

00:18:54.660 --> 00:18:56.730
- I'm gonna ask you to
continue on this thread

00:18:56.730 --> 00:18:57.563
just a little bit,

00:18:57.563 --> 00:19:01.483
because I would imagine that
for many of the listeners here,

00:19:02.690 --> 00:19:06.040
this extension of Malayness to Japan

00:19:06.040 --> 00:19:07.693
might come as a surprise.

00:19:08.920 --> 00:19:10.630
I think it's possible to imagine

00:19:10.630 --> 00:19:14.139
when it looks at the
geography of the cartography,

00:19:14.139 --> 00:19:16.880
just the landscape of Southeast Asia

00:19:16.880 --> 00:19:19.600
to imagine the ways in
which the Malay constructed

00:19:19.600 --> 00:19:22.377
Malay identity and linguistic connections

00:19:22.377 --> 00:19:27.140
brings the Philippines
directly into that orbit.

00:19:27.140 --> 00:19:28.840
What are some of the ways

00:19:28.840 --> 00:19:31.649
in which this was being extended to Japan

00:19:31.649 --> 00:19:33.930
and who were some of the key actors

00:19:33.930 --> 00:19:38.420
doing this kind of this racialization

00:19:38.420 --> 00:19:40.893
and expansion of the
Malay world into Japan?

00:19:41.730 --> 00:19:44.720
- So the illustrators who are part

00:19:44.720 --> 00:19:46.300
of the propaganda movement,

00:19:46.300 --> 00:19:50.400
so sort of educated elite, the straddles

00:19:50.400 --> 00:19:53.118
like the national hero, Coursera Resel,

00:19:53.118 --> 00:19:56.990
Marianna Ponce, who becomes
the foreign Emissary to Japan

00:19:56.990 --> 00:19:58.793
under the first Philippine Republic,

00:19:59.890 --> 00:20:01.860
as well as their colleagues,

00:20:01.860 --> 00:20:04.700
Isabella de Los Reyes
and other illustrators.

00:20:04.700 --> 00:20:08.880
And they sort of do things like

00:20:08.880 --> 00:20:10.765
looking at similar folk tales

00:20:10.765 --> 00:20:14.180
and myths that the Japanese have,

00:20:14.180 --> 00:20:15.890
that the Filipinos also have,

00:20:15.890 --> 00:20:18.850
and that their neighbors do.

00:20:18.850 --> 00:20:21.980
So they're very crude

00:20:23.730 --> 00:20:26.630
sort of extensions and comparisons,

00:20:26.630 --> 00:20:28.313
but they try to assert,

00:20:29.650 --> 00:20:32.550
I think of kind of fantasy

00:20:32.550 --> 00:20:35.600
of shared sort of

00:20:37.050 --> 00:20:39.320
race with the Japanese

00:20:39.320 --> 00:20:41.710
and at the same time,

00:20:41.710 --> 00:20:44.130
as they try to assert this,

00:20:44.130 --> 00:20:45.860
it's on the one hand,

00:20:45.860 --> 00:20:48.917
a kind of strategy argue to the Spanish

00:20:48.917 --> 00:20:50.170
to basically say,

00:20:50.170 --> 00:20:52.460
make good on your civilizing mission

00:20:52.460 --> 00:20:55.200
and give us the reforms where requesting,

00:20:55.200 --> 00:20:58.940
because we have this affinity with Japan

00:20:59.820 --> 00:21:04.460
and through the inalienability of place,

00:21:04.460 --> 00:21:07.280
it's almost inevitable that
we will join up with them.

00:21:07.280 --> 00:21:08.930
And as they arise,

00:21:08.930 --> 00:21:10.220
they are a greater threat to you.

00:21:10.220 --> 00:21:11.330
So if you want to safeguard

00:21:11.330 --> 00:21:13.484
your one of your final global holdings,

00:21:13.484 --> 00:21:15.660
give us the reforms we're asking for.

00:21:15.660 --> 00:21:17.090
So this is a little tongue in cheek there,

00:21:17.090 --> 00:21:18.233
but at the same time,

00:21:19.650 --> 00:21:22.120
they also think of it as a bit of a fat

00:21:22.120 --> 00:21:23.881
as a sort of dream, right?

00:21:23.881 --> 00:21:26.650
And it's like it's an inspiration.

00:21:26.650 --> 00:21:29.190
And when you read the writings

00:21:29.190 --> 00:21:31.520
where they're claiming
that the Japanese are Malay

00:21:31.520 --> 00:21:32.960
usually they actually
just say it like that.

00:21:32.960 --> 00:21:33.793
They don't have to prove it.

00:21:33.793 --> 00:21:36.280
They just say the Japanese are Malay.

00:21:36.280 --> 00:21:37.553
They are noble Malay.

00:21:39.150 --> 00:21:43.440
They also construct Japan,

00:21:43.440 --> 00:21:47.610
not as this sort of
really familiar entity,

00:21:47.610 --> 00:21:50.470
but as this almost kind
of like alternate future

00:21:50.470 --> 00:21:53.387
and this alternate future,

00:21:53.387 --> 00:21:54.900
not just for the Philippines,

00:21:54.900 --> 00:21:56.530
but more globally,

00:21:56.530 --> 00:22:00.710
the way that sort of our Socratic

00:22:02.110 --> 00:22:02.980
noble titles,

00:22:02.980 --> 00:22:06.390
aren't given up in a single breath

00:22:06.390 --> 00:22:09.360
and it go voluntarily

00:22:09.360 --> 00:22:13.660
the sort kind of discipline
and self-discipline

00:22:13.660 --> 00:22:14.810
that they exhibit

00:22:14.810 --> 00:22:17.410
they're held up as a thing
apart from history actually,

00:22:17.410 --> 00:22:19.120
and quite exceptional and not something

00:22:19.120 --> 00:22:22.870
that I quite like in terms of kind.

00:22:22.870 --> 00:22:25.403
And so they played both
games at the same time,

00:22:26.750 --> 00:22:28.530
or maybe more accurately.

00:22:28.530 --> 00:22:30.830
They try to assert a kind of similarity

00:22:30.830 --> 00:22:31.870
while at the same time,

00:22:31.870 --> 00:22:34.640
what they actually say seems to betray

00:22:34.640 --> 00:22:35.940
that they think of Japan

00:22:35.940 --> 00:22:37.350
as something very different

00:22:37.350 --> 00:22:40.143
and very dissimilar from everything else.

00:22:44.780 --> 00:22:45.613
- Interesting.

00:22:45.613 --> 00:22:48.004
I wanna explore this connection to Japan

00:22:48.004 --> 00:22:51.740
through one of the key
examples that you use

00:22:51.740 --> 00:22:53.600
in the middle of the book.

00:22:53.600 --> 00:22:58.340
You draw this parallel between
the Philippine revolution

00:22:58.340 --> 00:23:02.940
and Vietnamese intellectuals
at the turn of the century,

00:23:02.940 --> 00:23:07.140
Fanbo Joe, Fantuchen especially Fanbo Joe

00:23:07.140 --> 00:23:11.160
and his travels to Japan
and the dounzu movement.

00:23:11.160 --> 00:23:12.420
And I have two questions.

00:23:12.420 --> 00:23:13.660
One is I'd like for you,

00:23:13.660 --> 00:23:17.360
just to kind of explain
that to the audience, right?

00:23:17.360 --> 00:23:19.770
Where you see the parallel.

00:23:19.770 --> 00:23:21.720
But the question I have for you is,

00:23:21.720 --> 00:23:24.760
to what degree is this a kind of parallel

00:23:24.760 --> 00:23:26.730
that's going through Japan,

00:23:26.730 --> 00:23:28.520
like at least two parallel crosses.

00:23:28.520 --> 00:23:30.130
You have the Philippines and Vietnam,

00:23:30.130 --> 00:23:34.146
both experiencing this kind of
engagement with Pan-Asianism

00:23:34.146 --> 00:23:37.620
resolve wants to create
a school in Hong Kong.

00:23:37.620 --> 00:23:40.370
It's very similar to Fanbo Joe's idea

00:23:40.370 --> 00:23:43.433
of the daunzu movement and the
studying in Japan movement.

00:23:46.040 --> 00:23:48.130
A parallel that kind of has a vector

00:23:48.130 --> 00:23:51.060
through places like Japan and Hong Kong,

00:23:51.060 --> 00:23:55.440
or is there actually kind
of cross conversations

00:23:55.440 --> 00:23:58.078
that are happening between
people like Fanboy Joe

00:23:58.078 --> 00:24:00.030
and Jose resolve as well?

00:24:00.030 --> 00:24:02.220
Like, so are there movements across

00:24:02.220 --> 00:24:03.900
or do they always go to sort of like

00:24:03.900 --> 00:24:05.960
a hub and then radiate outward?

00:24:05.960 --> 00:24:08.980
And how do you imagine that Pan-Asianism

00:24:08.980 --> 00:24:11.023
sort of working in that way?

00:24:13.260 --> 00:24:17.560
- So, we don't have any records of,

00:24:17.560 --> 00:24:18.880
for example, Marianna Ponce,

00:24:18.880 --> 00:24:21.163
who was in Japan as the
Philippine Emissary,

00:24:22.030 --> 00:24:26.183
no records of him having any
meeting with someone which are,

00:24:27.740 --> 00:24:31.873
or any of his colleagues in Japan.

00:24:33.230 --> 00:24:36.110
Though we have a very long record

00:24:36.110 --> 00:24:37.890
of him with Sonia Said,

00:24:37.890 --> 00:24:39.810
Yanchi Chan and others.

00:24:39.810 --> 00:24:44.030
So unfortunately we don't have that.

00:24:44.030 --> 00:24:46.880
But what we do have are
sort of associations,

00:24:46.880 --> 00:24:48.050
Spanish associations,

00:24:48.050 --> 00:24:50.400
Japan is where we see
that there are Vietnamese

00:24:50.400 --> 00:24:51.250
and Siamese

00:24:51.250 --> 00:24:53.840
and Filipinos and Koreans all as members.

00:24:53.840 --> 00:24:57.040
So, but we don't know the extent to which

00:24:57.040 --> 00:24:58.230
that was a fruitful engagement,

00:24:58.230 --> 00:24:59.280
how productive it was,

00:24:59.280 --> 00:25:01.390
who was speaking to whom

00:25:01.390 --> 00:25:03.970
with what kind of regularity or depth.

00:25:03.970 --> 00:25:05.913
But we do know that that did exist.

00:25:08.230 --> 00:25:10.503
So I do see them as parallel,

00:25:11.710 --> 00:25:15.700
though they may have been more connected

00:25:15.700 --> 00:25:17.623
than we're able to see currently.

00:25:18.670 --> 00:25:20.500
One of the things I think is different

00:25:20.500 --> 00:25:22.070
between Vietnam and the Philippines

00:25:22.070 --> 00:25:24.400
within this sort of trans nationalization

00:25:24.400 --> 00:25:25.580
of Pan Asia,

00:25:25.580 --> 00:25:28.475
is that Vietnam,

00:25:28.475 --> 00:25:30.550
at least in the way that I conceive a bit,

00:25:30.550 --> 00:25:33.010
which is there's the sort of the center

00:25:33.010 --> 00:25:35.130
of the pagination discourse,

00:25:35.130 --> 00:25:36.560
which is the sort of cynic center

00:25:36.560 --> 00:25:38.600
emanating out of Japan and China.

00:25:38.600 --> 00:25:40.340
And then there's the periphery.

00:25:40.340 --> 00:25:43.710
And most of the traditional
literature discounts,

00:25:43.710 --> 00:25:45.433
peripheral Pan Asianism,

00:25:46.440 --> 00:25:50.540
seeing it as sort of
only instrumentalizing

00:25:50.540 --> 00:25:52.410
Pan-Asianism toward what are ultimately

00:25:52.410 --> 00:25:53.810
nationalists priorities.

00:25:53.810 --> 00:25:58.347
And so therefore discounting
them as true Pan Asianists.

00:26:00.285 --> 00:26:02.920
and I'm trying to sort of make the

00:26:02.920 --> 00:26:05.780
peripheral Pan Asianism
legible to the center,

00:26:05.780 --> 00:26:08.950
looking at different dimensions to it.

00:26:08.950 --> 00:26:12.720
But Vietnam is sort of in between the two,

00:26:12.720 --> 00:26:15.331
because it is traditionally
part of the cynic world,

00:26:15.331 --> 00:26:17.880
but it is also distinct

00:26:17.880 --> 00:26:19.250
and it is part of Southeast Asia.

00:26:19.250 --> 00:26:21.070
So it's almost like a stepping stone.

00:26:21.070 --> 00:26:22.380
And so it's an interesting way

00:26:22.380 --> 00:26:23.650
in which you can test

00:26:24.650 --> 00:26:26.270
sort of constructions of Asia,

00:26:26.270 --> 00:26:28.320
how far they have to change

00:26:28.320 --> 00:26:33.090
and be reinterpreted in
order to be transnationalized

00:26:33.090 --> 00:26:34.270
and where that's effective

00:26:34.270 --> 00:26:36.853
and where it isn't it
through what methods.

00:26:36.853 --> 00:26:39.820
So, it's an interesting comparison

00:26:41.891 --> 00:26:42.724
but it is different.

00:26:42.724 --> 00:26:44.470
And I think the scales are different.

00:26:45.330 --> 00:26:46.310
So for example,

00:26:46.310 --> 00:26:50.050
Vietnam doesn't have to
reimagine the Japanese is Molay

00:26:50.050 --> 00:26:53.430
by looking at a folk
tale about the tortoise.

00:26:53.430 --> 00:26:54.563
It's very different.

00:26:58.574 --> 00:27:00.620
And so I guess when you think about

00:27:00.620 --> 00:27:02.080
geographies of political affinity,

00:27:02.080 --> 00:27:04.830
which is one of the
things I was doing here,

00:27:04.830 --> 00:27:06.030
there's less work there,

00:27:07.460 --> 00:27:08.600
but at the same time,

00:27:08.600 --> 00:27:11.840
I do think that

00:27:11.840 --> 00:27:14.140
Vietnam does have strong comparison

00:27:14.140 --> 00:27:15.250
to the Philippines

00:27:15.250 --> 00:27:18.870
because of the two aspects
of anti-colonialism

00:27:18.870 --> 00:27:20.220
and nationalism

00:27:20.220 --> 00:27:22.060
and the ways in which those are inflected

00:27:22.060 --> 00:27:26.440
by interpretations of social Darwinism,

00:27:26.440 --> 00:27:31.440
which then lead both to
strategies of kind of Asianism

00:27:32.043 --> 00:27:37.043
and affinities toward an
idea of Asian as solidarity.

00:27:39.100 --> 00:27:41.019
