00:55:53.970 --> 00:55:54.820
Go ahead, Nati.

00:55:54.820 --> 00:55:56.780
Thank you for your patience.

00:55:56.780 --> 00:55:58.120
- Thank you. And thanks, Nicole.

00:55:58.120 --> 00:55:59.931
Both for the discussion on your book.

00:55:59.931 --> 00:56:02.760
So I'm a film historian
from Stockholm University.

00:56:02.760 --> 00:56:06.840
So the issues I raised is both
connected to issues raised by

00:56:06.840 --> 00:56:08.550
Eric, but also to early cinema.

00:56:08.550 --> 00:56:11.410
And I would like to hear
your thoughts on this,

00:56:11.410 --> 00:56:13.630
because I think it resonates
quite a lot with what you said.

00:56:13.630 --> 00:56:16.750
So on the topic of like
Pan-Asian ism in Japan.

00:56:16.750 --> 00:56:19.050
So according to newspaper
reports from Singapore

00:56:19.050 --> 00:56:23.370
after the screening of Russia
Japanese war films in 1905,

00:56:23.370 --> 00:56:25.920
the audience stood up and
applauded after seeing

00:56:25.920 --> 00:56:27.230
the Japanese win

00:56:27.230 --> 00:56:28.527
due to like a shared sense of Asia

00:56:28.527 --> 00:56:30.076
and as serenity colonialness.

00:56:30.076 --> 00:56:33.150
I was wondering if it was anything similar

00:56:33.150 --> 00:56:35.339
in the Philippines and a similar reception

00:56:35.339 --> 00:56:38.390
or thoughts on the Russia Japanese war.

00:56:38.390 --> 00:56:40.520
And then the second one is
about this issue of shared

00:56:40.520 --> 00:56:43.140
Malayness that we also discussed
in the beginning of this

00:56:43.140 --> 00:56:43.973
seminar.

00:56:43.973 --> 00:56:45.800
So the first Filipino film company

00:56:47.820 --> 00:56:51.130
was and movies formed in 1917.

00:56:51.130 --> 00:56:53.240
And I know that is after
your period of time,

00:56:53.240 --> 00:56:54.073
but I was wondering,

00:56:54.073 --> 00:56:58.080
was it common in the popular
imagination to use Molay in

00:56:58.080 --> 00:57:00.093
that sense in the Philippines.

00:57:02.420 --> 00:57:04.670
- Thank you for your questions.

00:57:04.670 --> 00:57:08.860
So the same moment that you
relate on sort of the research

00:57:08.860 --> 00:57:10.449
Japanese war film,

00:57:10.449 --> 00:57:12.010
when people standing up and sharing,

00:57:12.010 --> 00:57:12.911
we have the same kind of moments

00:57:12.911 --> 00:57:15.760
recorded in the Philippines where,

00:57:15.760 --> 00:57:18.430
you have sort of university students

00:57:18.430 --> 00:57:19.990
writing about it in the papers

00:57:19.990 --> 00:57:22.280
and running out and cheering,

00:57:22.280 --> 00:57:24.560
but at the same time actually,

00:57:24.560 --> 00:57:28.900
whereas the recent Japanese
war was such a inspiration

00:57:28.900 --> 00:57:31.430
in Southeast Asia,

00:57:31.430 --> 00:57:36.430
actually in both French new
China and the Philippines,

00:57:36.540 --> 00:57:38.490
it's the Sino Japanese were

00:57:38.490 --> 00:57:41.829
a little bit earlier that actually was

00:57:41.829 --> 00:57:44.880
more inspiring and more story

00:57:45.750 --> 00:57:48.400
in a really sort of expanded

00:57:48.400 --> 00:57:50.500
the political imaginaries.

00:57:50.500 --> 00:57:53.283
Because by the time you get
to the Russo-Japanese war,

00:57:54.530 --> 00:57:58.310
you start to see Japan acting
more like the Western Imperial

00:57:58.310 --> 00:58:02.170
powers than necessarily
the Asian savior to come.

00:58:02.170 --> 00:58:06.210
And those at the Vietnamese
and the Filipinos

00:58:06.210 --> 00:58:08.690
who had been watching
a little more closely

00:58:08.690 --> 00:58:11.670
and I think were a little earlier on in

00:58:11.670 --> 00:58:14.840
their anti-colonial sort of nationalisms

00:58:14.840 --> 00:58:17.380
and had more direct relationship

00:58:17.380 --> 00:58:20.920
with sort of this trans
naturalization upon Asia

00:58:20.920 --> 00:58:23.540
saw firsthand the ways in which

00:58:24.380 --> 00:58:29.380
Japan's geopolitical
and national priorities

00:58:29.690 --> 00:58:33.900
and ambitions could
lead them to collaborate

00:58:33.900 --> 00:58:35.910
with powers like France, for example.

00:58:35.910 --> 00:58:37.463
And they would extradite

00:58:37.463 --> 00:58:39.940
a lot of the Vietnamese would come

00:58:39.940 --> 00:58:41.420
under the Damvu movement

00:58:41.420 --> 00:58:43.290
to learn directly from Japan.

00:58:43.290 --> 00:58:46.600
So the timeline is a little bit different,

00:58:46.600 --> 00:58:47.840
I think in the Philippines

00:58:47.840 --> 00:58:51.040
and in Vietnam than it is
elsewhere in Southeast Asia.

00:58:51.040 --> 00:58:51.873
There's also,

00:58:52.710 --> 00:58:56.370
some discussion in the
literature about welfare.

00:58:56.370 --> 00:58:58.330
The Russo-Japanese war

00:58:58.330 --> 00:59:01.890
was such a big shock

00:59:01.890 --> 00:59:04.860
to Asian observers,

00:59:04.860 --> 00:59:06.900
and really maybe it was such a big shock

00:59:06.900 --> 00:59:11.290
to Western observers to
see an Asian power become

00:59:11.290 --> 00:59:13.030
a modern world power.

00:59:13.030 --> 00:59:17.095
Whereas actually maybe
for the Thai or the Molay,

00:59:17.095 --> 00:59:20.060
it seemed a little bit more natural

00:59:21.050 --> 00:59:25.773
that modern power would
emerge in Asia that was Asian,

00:59:25.773 --> 00:59:29.480
and that maybe some of the interpretations

00:59:29.480 --> 00:59:34.473
and are colored by Western
observers, total shock.

00:59:35.570 --> 00:59:36.690
And then

00:59:36.690 --> 00:59:40.500
I can't remember now which book it was

00:59:40.500 --> 00:59:41.400
if it was Paul brother,

00:59:41.400 --> 00:59:43.130
but I think he, he makes an argument,

00:59:43.130 --> 00:59:45.980
which I don't ascribe to.

00:59:45.980 --> 00:59:47.930
Which is basically that it depended on

00:59:47.930 --> 00:59:50.920
the state of development of the different

00:59:52.300 --> 00:59:54.980
Southeast Asian nations politically

00:59:54.980 --> 00:59:57.880
in terms of how they interpreted
the recent Japanese war.

00:59:57.880 --> 01:00:00.440
But I don't ascribe to that,

01:00:00.440 --> 01:00:01.310
but I just bring it up

01:00:01.310 --> 01:00:04.337
to point to some of the discussions

01:00:04.337 --> 01:00:06.400
and literature about the interpretation

01:00:09.250 --> 01:00:11.233
and what might be the misinterpretation.

01:00:25.482 --> 01:00:27.065
- Sorry about that.

01:00:29.360 --> 01:00:30.360
- You're muted Eric.

01:00:32.090 --> 01:00:33.413
- It's double muted.

01:00:35.970 --> 01:00:37.221
Nicole.

01:00:37.221 --> 01:00:38.950
What I was going to ask is how
are you feeling energy wise?

01:00:38.950 --> 01:00:40.630
Do you have time for one more question

01:00:40.630 --> 01:00:43.288
or would you like to end here.

01:00:43.288 --> 01:00:47.378
There's one or two possibilities?

01:00:47.378 --> 01:00:48.211
- Yeah.

01:00:48.211 --> 01:00:49.850
I think I was feeling worse
actually in the beginning

01:00:49.850 --> 01:00:51.180
where I felt that was
just sort of babbling

01:00:51.180 --> 01:00:52.020
and not making sense.

01:00:52.020 --> 01:00:55.460
And now I'm almost getting
my (sound fading away)

01:00:55.460 --> 01:00:56.771
So yeah.

01:00:56.771 --> 01:00:58.040
I think maybe two more questions

01:00:58.040 --> 01:01:00.233
now that I'm a little bit more coherent.

01:01:01.540 --> 01:01:05.630
- So Chang, are you still there?

01:01:05.630 --> 01:01:07.713
You had a question and you, yeah.

01:01:07.713 --> 01:01:09.600
Go ahead and ask your question

01:01:09.600 --> 01:01:12.230
and if Alyssa Paredes is
still has a question,

01:01:12.230 --> 01:01:15.390
we'll let her ask otherwise
we'll stop at this.

01:01:15.390 --> 01:01:17.200
- Yeah, I think just so much.

01:01:17.200 --> 01:01:20.640
Nicole, it' so nice to share
your talk about this in person.

01:01:20.640 --> 01:01:23.390
It's 2:00 AM here, as well
as like you sympathize.

01:01:25.226 --> 01:01:27.220
I have a question about

01:01:28.500 --> 01:01:31.820
world making and worlds and skills.

01:01:31.820 --> 01:01:35.437
So, you've talked a lot
about kind of the local also

01:01:35.437 --> 01:01:36.520
the global

01:01:36.520 --> 01:01:38.180
and from your book,

01:01:38.180 --> 01:01:41.770
a what really struck me was
the idea that this space

01:01:41.770 --> 01:01:43.190
that we now call Southeast Asia

01:01:43.190 --> 01:01:44.920
had a lot of different scales

01:01:44.920 --> 01:01:47.130
interacting at the same time.

01:01:47.130 --> 01:01:49.960
You've got different
worlds being constructed.

01:01:49.960 --> 01:01:50.793
So,

01:01:50.793 --> 01:01:52.980
and that really comes through
in the story that you tell.

01:01:52.980 --> 01:01:54.053
So I'm curious,

01:01:55.730 --> 01:01:58.070
what led you to ground the discussion

01:01:58.070 --> 01:02:02.290
in the world that that was grounded

01:02:02.290 --> 01:02:04.580
by this idea of Pan Asianism,

01:02:04.580 --> 01:02:06.130
rather than all these other worlds

01:02:06.130 --> 01:02:07.932
that people were constructing,

01:02:07.932 --> 01:02:10.290
the specific subjects
that you are looking at,

01:02:10.290 --> 01:02:12.453
what were themselves constructing as well?

01:02:17.139 --> 01:02:17.972
- So I think

01:02:17.972 --> 01:02:19.880
there were a lot of sort
of discrepant threads

01:02:19.880 --> 01:02:21.871
and in the book and

01:02:21.871 --> 01:02:24.980
for those here who know what
my dissertation was like,

01:02:24.980 --> 01:02:26.670
they were much more discrepant

01:02:26.670 --> 01:02:29.650
and much more varied in
the dissertation stuff.

01:02:29.650 --> 01:02:32.590
And I think that this construction of Asia

01:02:32.590 --> 01:02:34.850
and the idea of Asia was the way in which

01:02:34.850 --> 01:02:36.650
I was able to thread

01:02:36.650 --> 01:02:38.690
lots of different discussions

01:02:38.690 --> 01:02:41.837
and different levels and different actors.

01:02:41.837 --> 01:02:46.520
And Asia allowed me to move
outward from the Philippines

01:02:47.920 --> 01:02:50.576
to see sort of comparisons.

01:02:50.576 --> 01:02:51.900
As I said before,

01:02:51.900 --> 01:02:54.750
Asia was the framework in which
a lot of Asian nationalism

01:02:54.750 --> 01:02:58.360
sort of arrayed their
nationalism and buttress then

01:02:58.360 --> 01:03:01.107
because of the sort of
social Darwinist framework

01:03:01.107 --> 01:03:03.920
at the time that were using

01:03:05.812 --> 01:03:08.730
and having that as my thread.

01:03:08.730 --> 01:03:11.640
And my emphasis allowed me to

01:03:11.640 --> 01:03:14.780
bring certain aspects of the
discourse on civilization,

01:03:14.780 --> 01:03:15.650
on race,

01:03:15.650 --> 01:03:16.790
on place,

01:03:16.790 --> 01:03:17.623
on nation,

01:03:17.623 --> 01:03:19.040
on belonging

01:03:19.040 --> 01:03:23.230
into the four and leave apart others.

01:03:23.230 --> 01:03:25.880
And it allowed me to
connect different actors

01:03:25.880 --> 01:03:28.300
in different threads through
this one central idea

01:03:28.300 --> 01:03:30.950
of the sort of creating the idea of Asia.

01:03:30.950 --> 01:03:33.090
But of course, the idea of Asia itself,

01:03:33.090 --> 01:03:35.810
once you break it down within
the Philippians itself,

01:03:35.810 --> 01:03:40.110
isn't a uniform homogenous
sort of category or space.

01:03:40.110 --> 01:03:41.810
And so then all these other scales

01:03:41.810 --> 01:03:42.810
come into play because

01:03:42.810 --> 01:03:44.240
sometimes when they talk about Asia,

01:03:44.240 --> 01:03:45.960
they're talking about the Malays.

01:03:45.960 --> 01:03:48.150
Sometimes they're talking about Japan.

01:03:48.150 --> 01:03:53.053
Sometimes they're talking
about all of oppressed,

01:03:54.880 --> 01:03:56.762
the non-West essentially.

01:03:56.762 --> 01:04:01.762
And I found that the kind of
formlessness of Asia itself

01:04:03.070 --> 01:04:06.143
was one of the things that
was most captivating because,

01:04:07.370 --> 01:04:12.150
it's like an index of the
power structures in the region,

01:04:12.150 --> 01:04:14.393
both in terms of fantasies and fears.

01:04:20.610 --> 01:04:21.443
- Thank you, Nicole.

01:04:21.443 --> 01:04:26.340
So we will conclude with our
final question from Lisa Perez,

01:04:26.340 --> 01:04:29.420
who is in a distinguished
line of Philippine scholars

01:04:29.420 --> 01:04:32.170
that can connect the both of you

01:04:32.170 --> 01:04:36.560
through the Yale program
in Southeast Asian studies.

01:04:36.560 --> 01:04:39.530
So it's a fitting way to
have our final question.

01:04:39.530 --> 01:04:40.453
Go ahead, Lisa.

01:04:41.440 --> 01:04:42.550
- Thanks so much for that. Erik,

01:04:42.550 --> 01:04:44.820
it's such a pleasure to
be able to join this group

01:04:44.820 --> 01:04:46.920
somewhat promiscuously.

01:04:46.920 --> 01:04:48.900
And Nicole, congratulations on this book,

01:04:48.900 --> 01:04:50.490
how wonderful.

01:04:50.490 --> 01:04:53.580
This is really a wonderful contribution

01:04:53.580 --> 01:04:54.450
to the literature

01:04:54.450 --> 01:04:57.060
and congratulations for being able to

01:04:57.060 --> 01:05:00.610
just like articulate
everything so forcefully,

01:05:00.610 --> 01:05:02.850
and so clearly at two
o'clock in the morning

01:05:02.850 --> 01:05:05.063
where you are, that's really something.

01:05:06.172 --> 01:05:08.774
So I really think that

01:05:08.774 --> 01:05:12.040
the idea to the book is
so it's just so inventive.

01:05:12.040 --> 01:05:14.970
And I was thinking about it
really speaks to something

01:05:14.970 --> 01:05:17.120
that hits close to home.

01:05:17.120 --> 01:05:18.760
And I'm an anthropologist.

01:05:18.760 --> 01:05:21.140
So I think somewhat
ethnographically about these things.

01:05:21.140 --> 01:05:23.304
And it reminded me of

01:05:23.304 --> 01:05:25.990
a little encounter that I had

01:05:25.990 --> 01:05:29.770
on a tok tok in Chiang Mai in Thailand.

01:05:29.770 --> 01:05:32.549
And the like Tokyo driver
was like asking me,

01:05:32.549 --> 01:05:34.577
where are you from?

01:05:34.577 --> 01:05:35.710
And I was like, Oh,

01:05:35.710 --> 01:05:36.810
I'm from the Philippines.

01:05:36.810 --> 01:05:39.330
And he was like, Oh, so we're neighbors.

01:05:39.330 --> 01:05:41.440
And then at that moment, I was just like,

01:05:41.440 --> 01:05:43.190
realized to myself,

01:05:43.190 --> 01:05:46.010
like realize that I had never thought

01:05:46.010 --> 01:05:49.100
of Thailand as my neighbor.

01:05:49.100 --> 01:05:51.800
I never grew up thinking
of Thailand in that way.

01:05:51.800 --> 01:05:53.080
So I think, I mean,

01:05:53.080 --> 01:05:56.502
that speaks just as much to
my own kind of parochialism

01:05:56.502 --> 01:06:00.500
as it does to, I thinkthe
kind of intellectual

01:06:00.500 --> 01:06:05.409
intuition that's at the core of the book.

01:06:05.409 --> 01:06:06.730
So needless to say,

01:06:06.730 --> 01:06:08.330
a lot of us are excited about it.

01:06:08.330 --> 01:06:09.690
I just wanted to ask

01:06:09.690 --> 01:06:11.610
like somewhat selfishly

01:06:13.480 --> 01:06:15.860
in sort of in the spirit
of Erik's question.

01:06:15.860 --> 01:06:19.323
Like if you have a vision
for Philippine study is,

01:06:22.570 --> 01:06:25.180
Is there like a direction
in Philippine history

01:06:25.180 --> 01:06:28.030
or Philippine studies in general that

01:06:28.030 --> 01:06:30.630
you would like to see

01:06:30.630 --> 01:06:32.659
and feel free to answer it.

01:06:32.659 --> 01:06:35.222
I don't mean to make it sound like

01:06:35.222 --> 01:06:38.640
a qualifying exam question
or anything like that.

01:06:38.640 --> 01:06:40.760
But to answer selfishly,

01:06:40.760 --> 01:06:42.006
if you could be just like

01:06:42.006 --> 01:06:45.280
if you could decide like where
Philippines studies went,

01:06:45.280 --> 01:06:48.440
like where would you take it?

01:06:48.440 --> 01:06:49.653
I'm so curious.

01:06:50.760 --> 01:06:54.380
- I'm excited to see your
Alisa and thank you for that.

01:06:54.380 --> 01:06:55.639
Actually,

01:06:55.639 --> 01:06:57.560
this is a good segue to

01:06:57.560 --> 01:07:01.070
a history of graphical
intervention that my colleague,

01:07:01.070 --> 01:07:04.030
Anthony Medrano and I are
currently undertaking,

01:07:04.030 --> 01:07:06.020
which is to return the Philippines

01:07:06.020 --> 01:07:08.090
to the region of Southeast Asia,

01:07:08.090 --> 01:07:09.320
from which it has been historically

01:07:09.320 --> 01:07:10.410
or graphically separated.

01:07:10.410 --> 01:07:11.630
So right now

01:07:11.630 --> 01:07:14.210
we're co-editing a
special issue of a journal

01:07:14.210 --> 01:07:15.890
we haven't submitted yet.

01:07:15.890 --> 01:07:20.890
We're still editing of sort
of multidisciplinary works

01:07:20.940 --> 01:07:23.960
that look at the Philippines in this vain.

01:07:23.960 --> 01:07:27.473
And we have a great
collection of essays so far.

01:07:28.330 --> 01:07:32.800
Some which look at sort of
transnational architecture,

01:07:32.800 --> 01:07:37.338
others that look at
archeology and linguistics

01:07:37.338 --> 01:07:40.790
as well as sort of claims on Saba

01:07:40.790 --> 01:07:43.333
and the perennial state of Borneo.

01:07:45.320 --> 01:07:48.270
And there's also, I think,
another article on music.

01:07:48.270 --> 01:07:50.850
So there are lots of ways you can connect

01:07:50.850 --> 01:07:53.170
the Philippines back to Southeast Asia.

01:07:53.170 --> 01:07:57.500
There's no one on meter
meteorological, observant Doritos,

01:07:57.500 --> 01:07:59.730
and sort of across the region.

01:07:59.730 --> 01:08:01.060
So yeah, there are lots of ways.

01:08:01.060 --> 01:08:03.290
And what we're trying to do

01:08:03.290 --> 01:08:05.750
is to return to the
Philippines different features.

01:08:05.750 --> 01:08:07.540
So also if you know of anyone

01:08:07.540 --> 01:08:10.020
who's working on anything
within this vein,

01:08:10.020 --> 01:08:11.523
please do send me their names.

01:08:13.360 --> 01:08:15.280
